[size=16pt][b]THE APPLIER[/b][/size]
[i]Bitcoin Machine Code, days 41 to 57: the state of the project on 2026-10-07[/i]

The fourth report in the series. "21 FOR 21" covered 2026-08-11 to 2026-09-02. "The Measuring Equipment" covered 2026-09-02 to 2026-09-19, and "Run 28 vs Core" the clean benchmark pair that ended on 2026-09-21. This one runs from 2026-09-21 to 2026-10-07. It was compiled from the project's own records: the git history of [url=https://github.com/BobClawblaw/bitcoinmachinecode]https://github.com/BobClawblaw/bitcoinmachinecode[/url], the pull requests, the worklogs and the performance plan, docs/releases, docs/reports (the IBD and stage reports), docs/devlog (the deployment record and the incident notes), and the assistant's persistent memory notes. Every quote is verbatim from one of those, and every figure names where it came from. As before, the counts are true as of the date given and will drift.

[quote]"The win is concurrency, not a faster per-block apply."
[i]-- docs/reports/2026-10-05-logged-ibd-pair.md[/i][/quote]

[b]Contents[/b]
[list][li]PROLOGUE: WHERE THE LAST REPORTS STOPPED[/li][li]PART I: THE NUMBERS — what seventeen days added[/li][li]PART II: THE LINK — the benchmark that measured the wrong thing well[/li][li]PART III: THE OUTAGES — what a deploy proves[/li][li]PART IV: THE LOGGED PAIR — concurrency, not apply speed[/li][li]PART V: THE PLAN — making the applier faster than Core's[/li][li]PART VI: PARITY — what Core heard, and what it ignored[/li][li]PART VII: THE SCAR TISSUE — patterns, and the rules they left[/li][li]PART VIII: WHAT IS STILL OPEN[/li][li]APPENDIX A: THE NUMBERS[/li][/list]

[hr]
[size=14pt][b]PROLOGUE: WHERE THE LAST REPORTS STOPPED[/b][/size]

"21 FOR 21" ended on a node, written entirely in hand-crafted x86-64 assembly and C with no human-typed code, whose UTXO set was MuHash-identical to Bitcoin Core's. "The Measuring Equipment" spent seventeen days making the benchmarks believable. "Run 28 vs Core" gave the first clean number: a from-genesis mainnet sync in 18 h 24 m 02 s against an unpolled Core v31.1's 19 h 32 m 54 s, 5.9% in bmc's favour, the UTXO set identical at 968,025.

That result had a qualifier which turned out to matter more than the number. Both nodes were bound by the same ~88 Mbit/s link. A 5.9% margin on a link-bound race says the node is not slower than Core. It does not say how fast it is.

The short version of the state on 2026-10-07:

[list][li]Speed. On a repaired link and on Core's own download rules where it counts, bmc run 41 had every index at the tip in 3 h 48 m 22 s. Core v31.1 rerun #7, on the same machine, the same NVMe and the same indexes, took 9 h 50 m 04 s. That is 0.39 of Core's time, and bmc was faster in every one of the ten 100,000-block segments (docs/reports/2026-10-07-run41-vs-core7-stage-report.md).[/li][li]What the margin is made of. On 10-05 a fully logged pair showed that bmc's per-block apply was 23% slower than Core's, and that the whole lead came from downloading and applying at the same time. The next three days went into the applier. In run 41 it is 11% faster than Core's: 11,842 thread-seconds against 13,340.[/li][li]Correctness. Every full-sync capstone in the period matched Core: runs 28 to 41, and the first sync with [tt]assumevalid=0[/tt], in which every script was evaluated. The UTXO set of run 41 is MuHash-identical to Core's at 970,364.[/li][li]Memory. This is the category Core keeps. Run 41's PSS peaked at 75.6 GB against Core's 34.3 GB, with the heap at 32.2 GB against 12.0.[/li][li]Production. It was down twice: 23 hours on 10-02/03, and RPC alone for 2 h 17 m on 10-07. Each outage added a line to what "a verified deploy" means.[/li][li]The work. 107 merged pull requests and 193 non-merge commits since 2026-09-21, written by Claude Opus 5.5 and Claude Fable 5.1, with one human operator deciding, merging and deploying.[/li][/list]

The title is literal. "The applier" is the thread that connects each block to the UTXO set. For most of these seventeen days it was the thing nobody had measured properly. When it was measured, it was the thing that was slower than Core. By the end it was faster.

[hr]
[size=14pt][b]PART I: THE NUMBERS — what seventeen days added[/b][/size]

[size=12pt][b]Chapter 1: Counting[/b][/size]

Counted on [tt]main[/tt] from the last commit of 2026-09-21 ([tt]7c6a67fc[/tt], 17:58Z) to [tt]aa22622b[/tt] (PR #404), the method the previous reports used:

[table]
[tr][td][b]merged pull requests[/b][/td][td][b]107 (#291 to #404, with gaps; #405 open)[/b][/td][/tr]
[tr][td]non-merge commits[/td][td]193[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]files changed[/td][td]358, +35,769 / −2,322 lines[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]test programs ([tt]asm/tests/test_*.c[/tt])[/td][td]427 → 456[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]full mainnet syncs completed, bmc[/td][td]runs 29 to 34 and 37 to 41, plus the [tt]assumevalid=0[/tt] sync[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]full mainnet syncs completed, Core v31.1[/td][td]reruns #4 to #7; #2 and #3 cut short[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]production snapshots deployed[/td][td]46 ([tt]deploy-20260925b[/tt] to [tt]deploy-20261007a[/tt])[/td][/tr]
[/table]

The deployment count is from docs/devlog/DEPLOYMENT_HISTORY.md, backfilled on 10-07 from the commit each snapshot binary stamps into itself. Five production restarts in that window left no snapshot on disk at all, one snapshot was built from a dirty tree, and two were written and never run. The record says which.

[size=12pt][b]Chapter 2: Who wrote it[/b][/size]

From the [tt]Co-Authored-By[/tt] trailers of the 193 commits: Claude Opus 5.5 114 (45 of them in its 1M-context form), Claude Fable 5.1 67, Claude Opus 5 2, and 10 with no trailer. Opus 5.5 arrived on 09-24 and Opus 5 left the same week.

The operator's role did not change from the previous report: every merge to [tt]main[/tt] and every production deploy is the operator's decision. What changed is how often it was made. On 09-28 production took eight deploys in one day ([tt]a[/tt] to [tt]h[/tt]). On 10-06 four benchmark syncs ran back to back, and a dozen pull requests landed between them.

A second tree joined the work: [tt]bmc_osx[/tt], a macOS/AArch64 port kept as a branch of the same repository. It found four real defects in the shared code (Part VI) and was wrong four times about whether its fixes applied to x86 (Part VII).

[hr]
[size=14pt][b]PART II: THE LINK — the benchmark that measured the wrong thing well[/b][/size]

[size=12pt][b]Chapter 3: Run 29, and what a repeat is for (09-22)[/b][/size]

Run 29 repeated run 28 on the same commit against the same Core baseline: 18:28:54, 5.5% ahead, PASS at 968,154 (docs/reports/2026-09-22-run29-vs-core.md). Its purpose was the spread. Two bmc runs of the same code differed by 4 m 52 s, so a margin of an hour was real and a margin of five minutes was not.

Both runs were bound by the link: ~88 Mbit/s of peer traffic sustained, for both nodes, the whole way. On that link, how fast either node applied blocks hardly mattered.

[size=12pt][b]Chapter 4: The repair (09-28 to 09-29)[/b][/size]

On 09-28 the operator repaired the box's backhaul. On 09-29 from about 13:30Z it moved to a faster one, measured at 636 Mbit/s sustained inbound (docs/reports/2026-09-30-ibd-benchmarks-bmc-vs-core.md). Nothing in the node changed, and every number moved:

[table]
[tr][td][/td][td][b]old link (09-22)[/b][/td][td][b]repaired link[/b][/td][/tr]
[tr][td]bmc to 400,000[/td][td]92 min (run 29)[/td][td]24 min (run 30)[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]bmc to 968,987[/td][td]—[/td][td]4:54:30 (run 30)[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]Core to 968,987[/td][td]—[/td][td]11:01:00 (rerun #4)[/td][/tr]
[/table]

Run 30 was 2.24× faster than Core. On the old link it had been 1.06×. The eighteen-hour syncs of the previous report were the link's numbers. The implementations had been waiting on it equally.

[size=12pt][b]Chapter 5: A one-second mount, nine hours lost (09-29)[/b][/size]

Core rerun #2 started at 16:02Z on 09-28. At 01:40Z on 09-29 a test in a different project on the same host (the ClusterMan VM tests) ran [tt]mount -o ro … /mnt; umount /mnt[/tt], and the mount was up for about a second. The benchmark NVMe was mounted at [tt]/mnt/nvme8tb[/tt], under [tt]/mnt[/tt]. For that second, Core's block write saw a read-only filesystem. Core stopped with EROFS at 924,616 after 9 h 38 m 24 s.

Two rules came out of it: read the journal for mount and loop events before blaming a drive, and run timed datadirs from [tt]/srv/nvme8tb[/tt], a bind mount that no [tt]/mnt[/tt] mount can shadow (memory note [tt]mount-on-mnt-shadows-nvme8tb.md[/tt]). Rerun #3 was cut by the operator when the backhaul changed underneath it. Rerun #4 started at 13:54Z on the new link and became the baseline for the next week.

[size=12pt][b]Chapter 6: The even comparison, and what it cost (09-29 to 10-01)[/b][/size]

With the link out of the way, the differences in configuration were all that was left, and on 09-29 every setting was checked line by line (docs/devlog/BENCHMARKS.md, "The even comparison"). The list found three that favoured Core:

[list][li]Download peers. Runs 27 to 30 used 8, because "Core's is 8". Core's [tt]MAX_OUTBOUND_FULL_RELAY_CONNECTIONS[/tt] is 8, but during IBD Core downloads from every outbound peer that can serve blocks, which includes its two block-relay-only peers: ten (docs/CORE_DIVERGENCES.md). The register had read one constant and missed the other.[/li][li]The download window: Core requests no further than 1,024 blocks above its connected tip, while bmc's window was 4,096.[/li][li][tt]dbcache[/tt]: the default was 1024 MiB here, "a value no Core release defaults to". Core's is 450.[/li][/list]

All three were matched (#344, #347). Run 31 on the matched settings came in at 5:28:11, 2.01× Core #4. Against run 30 that was a loss, and the report named it: "the 4,096 window was worth 11.5% to bmc". A Core-sized window starved bmc's 40-block chunk requests. The fix was to stay at 1,024 and ask for less at once: 16-block chunks, A/B-tested to 300,000 blocks at 19.5% and 14.1% faster than 40 (#361, 10-01).

The same days produced the parity correction behind every later number. On 10-01 the project went to one Core oracle, the v31.1 release, on RPC 8335 (#364). The v31.99 development build that had answered on those paths was deleted. Its answers had invented work, because they differed from the version bmc targets.

[size=12pt][b]Chapter 7: Core's own rules (10-04)[/b][/size]

Matching Core's settings still left bmc's own download policy in the race: peers ranked by speed, slow peers rotated, a staller banned. That is a real feature, but it does not compare implementations. So on 10-04 bmc learned to download by Core's rules ([tt]bmc.dlshape=core[/tt], #380): 16 blocks in flight per peer, topped up as each lands, peers in random order, no ranking or rate floor, a staller disconnected and never banned, and Core's block download timeout. A per-block stage log came with it ([tt]bmc.benchlog[/tt]), which splits each block's apply into the same columns on both nodes. A [tt][ready][/tt] line marks the moment every index reaches the tip.

The comparison was now as even as the project could make it. Part IV is what it showed.

[hr]
[size=14pt][b]PART III: THE OUTAGES — what a deploy proves[/b][/size]

Production ran three incidents in this period. Each passed every check that existed at the time, and each added one.

[size=12pt][b]Chapter 8: 70 minutes on one block (09-25)[/b][/size]

PR #303 made bmc's legs ask for headers from the parent of the announced block, as Core does. It was deployed, checked for what it changed, and called good. On the next block, production's apply stalled for 70 minutes at 968,555.

The cause was two [tt]close()[/tt] calls on the same descriptor in the boot fill. The first closed it, the kernel reused the number for the store's cached block file, and the second closed the store's file. The Mac session's note found it (worklog/2026-09-25-note-for-x86.md), and #306 fixed it.

The rule: a deploy is verified when a new block arrives and is applied with the oracle's hash, not when the change works (memory note [tt]deploy-watch-the-next-block.md[/tt]).

[size=12pt][b]Chapter 9: 23 hours, and an address that named its bug (10-02 to 10-03)[/b][/size]

[tt]deploy-20261002a[/tt] applied block 969,530 correctly at about 04:00Z and was called verified under the new rule. At 04:02Z it segfaulted. It crashed again about 40 seconds after every restart, as soon as mempool.space re-synced through the Esplora facade. At 04:28Z systemd gave up, and nothing noticed until the next session, 23 hours later.

The cause was a call path that skipped a lock. #368's facade batch route called [tt]rpc_chain_tx_blockhash[/tt] without the execution lock while [tt]irs_refresh[/tt], on another thread, was zeroing the run maps it reads.

The address found it first. The unit runs with [tt]LimitCORE=0[/tt], because the decrypted wallet seed is in memory and a core file would put it on disk. There was no dump to open:

[quote]"All eleven crashes faulted at the same address, [tt]0x6a61e3bd8[/tt]. That address is exactly the run's sparse-index offset (48 + 1,425,612,630 × 20) plus the first binary-search probe (16 × 2,784,399) on top of a NULL base."
[i]-- docs/releases/2026-10-03-the-outage-and-cores-words.md[/i][/quote]

A constant address despite ASLR, equal to a data-derived offset, means a NULL pointer plus an index: a map read after it was cleared. #373 fixed the lock. #376 added a crash handler that prints the fault address, the registers and a backtrace to the log, which [tt]addr2line[/tt] resolves against the non-PIE binary, still without a core file. #379 fixed six more instances of the same three defect classes, found by auditing for them.

Two rules: one good block proves the apply path and nothing else, so a deploy also needs [tt]NRestarts=0[/tt] ten minutes in with the RPC clients reconnected; and anything that calls into the chain code directly must take the lock the RPC dispatcher would have taken (memory note [tt]lock-free-callers-bypass-the-exec-lock.md[/tt]).

[size=12pt][b]Chapter 10: The service was up and RPC was not (10-07)[/b][/size]

At 16:26:22Z on 10-07 production logged one line:

[code][rpc] accept: out of file descriptors (Too many open files) -- backing off; the RPC listener is degraded[/code]

and nothing watched for it. P2P sync carried on, the tip advanced, [tt]systemctl is-active[/tt] said active. The RPC port and the Esplora facade accepted nothing, and the mempool.space instance built on them stopped at 970,365. The operator asked "why is production down?" at 18:22Z.

[tt]/proc[/tt] had the answer: 1,023 of 1,024 descriptors open, 314 of them on [tt]index.dat[/tt], each paired with two block files. The getblock reader lane, part of the 10-05 work to stop RPC lock-ups (Part V), gave each thread a private store handle (three descriptors) and an 8 MB buffer, in thread-local storage, the first time it called [tt]getblock[/tt]. For the fixed pool of RPC workers that is a fixed cost. The Esplora facade is not a pool. It starts a thread per connection, and mempool.space reads blocks through it all day. Every one of those threads exited holding its lane.

The fix is a pthread key whose destructor releases the lane when its thread exits (#402). Its test runs 64 threads that each call [tt]getblock[/tt] once and exit, and checks that the descriptor count does not move. On the unfixed code it went from 14 to 206. Production was back at 18:43:46Z on [tt]deploy-20261007a[/tt], after 2 h 17 m. The descriptor count has stayed at 66 since. A fourth criterion joined the deploy check: the descriptor count stays flat across client traffic. That is a measurement over time, and the only one of the four that would have caught this (docs/devlog/INCIDENT_2026-10-07_reader_lane_fd_leak.md).

The 09-03 codebase audit had described this failure shape for a different leak, a month earlier: at the default limit of 1,024, "the RPC listener and the inbound P2P listener both stop accepting".

[hr]
[size=14pt][b]PART IV: THE LOGGED PAIR — concurrency, not apply speed[/b][/size]

[size=12pt][b]Chapter 11: Run 34 against Core rerun #6 (10-04 to 10-05)[/b][/size]

Core rerun #6 started at 18:20:24Z on 10-04 with the stage logging on. Run 34 followed on Core's download rules. Both reached the point where every index was at the tip:

[table]
[tr][td][/td][td][b]Core v31.1 rerun #6[/b][/td][td][b]bmc run 34[/b][/td][/tr]
[tr][td]ready[/td][td]10:42:05[/td][td]7:17:39[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]applier busy (s)[/td][td]13,909 (36%)[/td][td]17,166 (64%)[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]applier waiting (s)[/td][td]24,587 (64%)[/td][td]9,757 (36%)[/td][/tr]
[/table]

1.47× faster on equal download terms, and the reason was not where anyone had assumed. The report's second finding:

[quote]"The win is concurrency, not a faster per-block apply. Core serialises download and connection on one thread (13.9k busy + 24.6k waiting); bmc overlaps them (17.2k busy, 9.8k waiting, most of that in the last segment)."
[i]-- docs/reports/2026-10-05-logged-ibd-pair.md[/i][/quote]

Core runs [tt]ProcessNewBlock[/tt] on its message-handling thread, so downloading and applying take turns. bmc downloads in forked workers while the applier connects blocks, so both happen at once. That overlap was worth more than bmc's whole per-block deficit. And there was a deficit: the applier was 23% slower per block than Core's, mostly in writing to the UTXO store ([tt]put[/tt], 7,812 s of the 17,166).

The memory note written that day says how to talk about it:

[quote]"claim apply speed only with the stage row beside it"
[i]-- memory note ibd-win-is-concurrency-not-apply.md[/i][/quote]

[size=12pt][b]Chapter 12: The 78 minutes that were one deaf worker (10-05)[/b][/size]

The pair report blamed run 34's slow last segment on random peers: "download-bound under random peers". Run 35, launched to check the next change, showed a different shape. One worker held the oldest missing chunk. The stall rule evicted it with SIGUSR1 twelve times, and it never acted on the signal. The window sat for seven minutes with nine workers idle at its edge and the applier at zero lag. Run 34's log had the same shape four times, about twenty minutes each, 78 minutes in all.

#384 made the first eviction hand the chunk to an idle worker straight away, as Core re-requests a disconnected staller's blocks. The fix's own diagnostic lines then misreported twice: one said "did not answer" with the worker's acknowledgement printed a line above, and another printed a stale "acknowledged late". #385 fixed the accounting. The memory note:

[quote]"check the [dlc wN] ack beside every stall line and block gaps > 60 s before calling a run download-bound"
[i]-- memory note eviction-is-a-signal-check-cursorhelp.md[/i][/quote]

[hr]
[size=14pt][b]PART V: THE PLAN — making the applier faster than Core's[/b][/size]

[size=12pt][b]Chapter 13: The ask (10-05)[/b][/size]

[quote]"resolve the rest of the performance holes and any rpc improvements so we don't lock up so much compared to core."
[i]-- the operator, worklog/2026-10-05-performance-holes-plan.md[/i][/quote]

The plan that answered it set numbers before work. Every item had to come with a measurement that moves, a test that fails with the change reverted, a full gate and a [tt]--no-ff[/tt] merge. The targets, and where they ended:

[table]
[tr][td][b]target[/b][/td][td][b]start (run 34)[/b][/td][td][b]target[/b][/td][td][b]run 41[/b][/td][/tr]
[tr][td]the applier (s)[/td][td]17,166[/td][td]≤ 12,000[/td][td]11,842[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]UTXO put (s)[/td][td]7,812[/td][td]≤ 4,500[/td][td]3,842[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]headers to block 1[/td][td]306 s[/td][td]≤ 90 s[/td][td]48 s[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]ready, ranked peers[/td][td]5:28[/td][td]≤ 4:45[/td][td]3:48:22[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]exec-lock waits ≥ 2 s, production[/td][td]~10 a day[/td][td]0[/td][td]0[/td][/tr]
[/table]

[size=12pt][b]Chapter 14: Taking work off the applier (10-05 to 10-06)[/b][/size]

In order of landing (each in docs/releases and the plan's status register):

[list][li]B1+B2 (#383): the undo record for a spent input reuses the prevout the validation phase had already resolved, instead of looking it up again. 2.30 → 1.13 µs per spent input on the bench; put 7,812 → 5,687 s in run 37.[/li][li]B5 (#383): the first header page is asked of four peers at once, and the fastest leads. Headers to block 1: 5:06 → 1:58.[/li][li]The RPC lanes (#383): the Esplora facade had taken the exclusive execution lock for every dispatch. Now it takes what the method's class needs. A txindex lane and a getblock reader lane serve the hot calls without the lock. Production's waits of 2 s or more went from about ten a day to none (and the reader lane is Chapter 10's leak).[/li][li]B4 (#390): txindex, the block filter and the address and spender tails are built by a forked index worker fed from a 1,024-slot ring: 688 s off the applier. Run 38: 4:17:09, 33 minutes faster than run 37.[/li][li]A7/A8 (#387, #388): [tt]getrawtransaction[/tt] at 32 concurrent clients went from 382 ms to 5 ms, Core's figure, and [tt]getdeploymentinfo[/tt], which re-walked about 10,000 headers per call under the exclusive lock, from 2 s to 4 ms.[/li][li]B8 (#395): the cumulative-work records are appended as the download goes instead of in one pass at the end. Run 38 had paused for about a minute there, on 970,000 cold 80-byte reads at queue depth 1. After B8 the pause was 0.55 s.[/li][li]B3 (#396): the UTXO memtable flush runs in a forked writer while the applier carries on. Flush time on the applier: 1,357 → 131 s. Building it found a latent defect in recovery: the reload's tombstone pass started at byte 0 instead of the checkpoint's offset.[/li][li]M1 (#394): the sampler had reported 26 GB of anonymous memory. It was summing copy-on-write pages once per forked child, 13 times. The real figure was about 10 GB.[/li][/list]

[size=12pt][b]Chapter 15: The same code, three different put times (B11)[/b][/size]

Three runs of the same put code reported three different put times: 4,994 s (run 38), 5,661 s (run 39), 7,135 s (run 40). Nothing in the put path had changed between them. The writer child of B3 was alive in run 40, so it was the obvious suspect. The per-input insert cost was the same with the writer alive and without it.

The tree's CPU time had risen only by the writer's own seconds. The extra 1,475 s had been spent waiting, not computing (plan, B11). The memtable's table and blob were [tt]MAP_SHARED[/tt] mappings of files: every insert dirtied a file page the kernel would write back, and on a box with 8 GB of swap full and 85 GB of page cache, a reclaimed page came back from the file on the next touch. A file-backed mapping also gets no transparent huge pages, so a 1.6 GB random-access table paid a TLB miss per probe.

Nothing read those files back. The boot and every tool rebuild their view from the WAL. So B11 (#400) moved the table and blob into anonymous memory with [tt]MADV_HUGEPAGE[/tt]. The files are still created at full size, because the offline tools read their sizes, and the table's first page stays mapped from the file, because the transaction-validation snapshot checks the live count there. In run 41 put was 3,842 s against run 40's 7,135. The insert went from 2,621 s to 664 and the spent-output delete from 3,079 s to 1,822, "by changing nothing in the code that runs them" (the release report, §2).

Adding that column exposed an older error. The release report's "undo capture" figures for runs 38 to 40 (2,242 → 2,463 → 3,079 s) had been printed one column off. They were the delete column. Undo capture had been 921 to 979 s throughout and had never been the mover. The report now says so at every place the shifted label survived.

[size=12pt][b]Chapter 16: A two-second sleep (B9 part 2, 10-07)[/b][/size]

Run 40 was ahead of Core #7 by the end, and behind it at the start: 2.0× slower to 100,000 and 1.7× slower to 200,000. The first hypothesis was round trips on tiny blocks. A rolling per-block request mode was built to test it ([tt]bmc.dlcrollbelow[/tt]), and it A/B-tested 13% slower or level, so it was rejected.

Run 40's own log had a pattern in it. Below 100,000, chunk completions came in 99 bursts, 2.09 s apart, with nothing at all arriving in 76 of 210 seconds. The workers filled a 1,024-block window of tiny early blocks in about 0.3 s, then waited out the download loop's whole 2-second idle sleep before the window moved. The fix polls the committer's tip every 20 ms while it waits ([tt]c6884a22[/tt]). The gate line read [tt]idle waits cut short 1670 (B9)[/tt]. 100,000 came at 1:35 instead of 4:16.

B10 landed in the same PR. Right after run 40's download, two legs claimed a height of 975,945 on a 970,229 chain. That started a full re-probe and re-ranking of 161 peers for 37 real blocks. A claim more than 50 blocks plus a block a minute above the pool's last median is no longer believed, and the tail went from 99 s to 34.

[size=12pt][b]Chapter 17: Run 41[/b][/size]

Run 41 launched at 12:52Z on 10-07 on [tt]7027c734[/tt], the commit with B9 part 2, B10, B11 and the B3 reap. From the stage report:

[table]
[tr][td][b]height[/b][/td][td][b]Core v31.1 rerun #7[/b][/td][td][b]bmc run 41[/b][/td][td][b]bmc / Core[/b][/td][/tr]
