[tr][td]300,000[/td][td]15:30[/td][td]6:57[/td][td]0.45[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]500,000[/td][td]1:43:30[/td][td]44:25[/td][td]0.43[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]700,000[/td][td]4:18:21[/td][td]1:42:57[/td][td]0.40[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]900,000[/td][td]8:13:37[/td][td]3:10:02[/td][td]0.38[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]every index at the tip[/td][td]9:50:04[/td][td]3:48:22[/td][td]0.39[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]CPU, all processes[/td][td]13 h 15 m[/td][td]7 h 39 m[/td][td]0.58[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]the applier (thread-seconds)[/td][td]13,340[/td][td]11,842[/td][td]0.89[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]UTXO set[/td][td]MuHash at 970,333[/td][td]identical at 970,364[/td][td][/td][/tr]
[/table]

Core #7 was itself 52 minutes faster than #6, all of it before 500,000. Its connect columns were within 4% of #6's, so "the difference is the peers it was given, not the box" (the release report). bmc was compared against the faster of the two.

The full progression of the release line against Core: run 34 0.68 on Core's download rules; runs 37 to 40 0.40 to 0.48 on bmc's; run 41 0.39.

[size=12pt][b]Chapter 18: The category Core keeps[/b][/size]

[table]
[tr][td][/td][td][b]Core #7[/b][/td][td][b]run 41[/b][/td][/tr]
[tr][td]heap, mean / peak[/td][td]10.0 / 12.0 GB[/td][td]15.3 / 32.2 GB[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]PSS, mean / peak[/td][td]17.6 / 34.3 GB[/td][td]39.9 / 75.6 GB[/td][/tr]
[/table]

Some of that is B11: the memtable's pages moved from the file-backed column to the anonymous one, counted once instead of hidden in page cache. The rest is the design. bmc keeps forked workers, a staged chunk store and a full memtable alongside each other, where Core keeps one process and a bounded cache. The release note states it plainly as the one category Core keeps, and [tt]bmc.memtableanon=0[/tt] restores the old mapping for anyone who wants the old trade.

[hr]
[size=14pt][b]PART VI: PARITY — what Core heard, and what it ignored[/b][/size]

[size=12pt][b]Chapter 19: No transaction we relayed ever reached Core (10-02)[/b][/size]

bmc's handshake sent BIP339 [tt]wtxidrelay[/tt], and its relay code announced transactions by txid ([tt]MSG_TX[/tt]). A comment said txid announcements were "universally understood". They are not:

[quote]"once wtxidrelay is negotiated, Core's net_processing ignores MSG_TX invs from that peer ("Ignore INVs that don't match wtxidrelay setting"). tx_relay.c and txann.c announce MSG_TX, so no transaction bmc relayed or originated ever reached a Core peer -- production included."
[i]-- commit 5c18b05c[/i][/quote]

It was seen on a regtest run where Core's [tt]getpeerinfo[/tt] showed 183 bytes of inv from bmc and no getdata back. The tests had asserted that [tt]wtxidrelay[/tt] was sent: they pinned the defect. The bumpfee end-to-end run went from 47 ok and 18 failed to 64 and 1. The next day bmc announced by [tt]MSG_WTX[/tt] properly and kept BIP339 (#374).

[size=12pt][b]Chapter 20: The compact blocks we served (09-25)[/b][/size]

[quote]"cmpctblock_build stored only the low byte of ntx-1 (`mov byte [r12+88], al`) with the short ids at +89, so for any block of 254 or more transactions -- nearly all of mainnet -- the cmpctblock served to an inbound peer (bitcoin_serve.asm:1084) was malformed"
[i]-- commit 64c5102b[/i][/quote]

The Mac port found it while porting the encoder, and the test it brought uses block 481,827 (1,377 transactions) as a fixture. On the receiving side a similar defect had been fixed on 09-09: a 5-byte compare of "block" had swallowed every [tt]blocktxn[/tt] for three days. Compact blocks are a protocol where the sender is rarely checked, because the receiver falls back to the full block and nothing visibly breaks.

[size=12pt][b]Chapter 21: Core's words, byte for byte (10-01 to 10-03)[/b][/size]

BlockYard, the project's block explorer, ran a mempool differential against Core and found bmc's pool missing children Core accepted (15 and 14 of 35, two of 246 clusters). That moved the mempool to Core v31.1's policy wholesale (#363): cluster limits only, v31.1's replacement rule with the feerate-diagram check, optimal linearization, 1p1c package RBF, fee floors that round up as v31.1's do. Rejections followed (#375, #377, #378). For 26 rejection cases, bmc now returns Core's exact reject text in Core's stage order, byte for byte.

[size=12pt][b]Chapter 22: Crypto parity, and two bench rows that flattered us (09-27 to 09-28)[/b][/size]

Every file in Core's [tt]src/bench[/tt] was classified and paired where bmc had a counterpart. The secp256k1 work (#319, #320) brought ECDSA verify to 20.7 µs and BIP340 to 22.4 µs, against libsecp256k1's 21.0 and 22.0. Asm ports of the modules (#329 to #334) took MuHash finalize from 1,861 µs, 66× behind Core, to 27.9 µs against Core's 28.0.

Two rows were published too well. MuHash's inverse was posted as "1.18× ahead", and a multiply as "7.7×". Decomposed, Core's benchmark loop does [tt]Finalize[/tt] plus a [tt]/=[/tt], two multiplies that bmc's row skipped, so the inverse was level. The multiply row compared one of bmc's multiplies against Core's [tt]*=[/tt], which runs two: the honest figure was 3.8×. The memory note:

[quote]"decompose both sides before claiming a win"
[i]-- memory note benchmark-rows-copy-cores-loop-shape.md[/i][/quote]

[size=12pt][b]Chapter 23: The first sync that checked every script (09-27)[/b][/size]

With [tt]assumevalid=0[/tt], bmc synced to the tip in 8 h 0 m at nice 10, evaluating every script, and the harness said FAIL at height 968,807. Recomputing the digest from the two accumulators by hand gave one SHA-256 whose forward hex was bmc's answer and whose reversed hex was Core's. A presentation change on 09-25 had flipped the byte order of the no-height [tt]gettxoutsetinfo[/tt] digest. The set had never been wrong (memory note [tt]gettxoutsetinfo-running-digest-is-wrong.md[/tt]). As with run 22's torn read in the previous report, a "UTXO set differs from Core" was the checker's fault, and suspecting the checker first found it in an afternoon.

[hr]
[size=14pt][b]PART VII: THE SCAR TISSUE — patterns, and the rules they left[/b][/size]

[size=12pt][b]Chapter 24: Per-something costs that were not per-something[/b][/size]

Two of this period's defects are one shape. The reader lane's cost was "per thread", which is fixed only if the threads are. The memory sampler's 26 GB was "per process", which double-counts only if the processes share pages, and they did, 13 times over. In both cases the unit of accounting was right for the design and wrong for one caller. The rule left behind is concrete: any thread-local resource needs a thread-exit release, or its callers must be a pool by construction. And count shared pages once.

[size=12pt][b]Chapter 25: The box as an adversary[/b][/size]

[list][li]A one-second mount by another project ended a nine-hour Core run (Chapter 5).[/li][li]A "stopped" smoke run's children kept syncing for 66 minutes beside Core rerun #6, which then had to be restarted, losing about 1.5 hours. "Stopped" now means no process has its working directory under the bench volume, and that is checked before every timed launch (memory note [tt]stopped-means-no-cwd-under-it.md[/tt]).[/li][li]The mempool.space backend died for eleven hours on 10-06 because its unit used upstream's development heap of 2,048 MB against a full mempool. It runs with 16 GB now.[/li][li]Swap was full for the whole release line, filled by a language-model server sharing the box. Every number in Part V was measured under that load, on both sides.[/li][/list]

[size=12pt][b]Chapter 26: The port that said "applies to x86"[/b][/size]

The [tt]bmc_osx[/tt] tree found real shared defects: the double close (Chapter 8), the CompactSize count (Chapter 20), three testnet4 fixes (#336), mutated compact blocks (#325). Its reports also said four times that a fix applied to x86 when it did not, and one race diagnosis named a thread that does not exist. Every port from it is now revert-checked on x86 and built with gcc [tt]-Werror[/tt] before it lands (memory note [tt]porting-bmc-osx-fixes-to-x86.md[/tt]).

[size=12pt][b]Chapter 27: The rules now in force[/b][/size]

Added in this period, each traceable to a chapter above:

[list type=decimal][li]A deploy is verified by four things: a new block applied with the oracle's hash; [tt]NRestarts=0[/tt] ten minutes in; the RPC clients reconnected; and the descriptor count flat across their traffic (Chapters 8 to 10).[/li][li]Anything that calls into the chain code directly takes the lock the RPC dispatcher would have taken (Chapter 9).[/li][li]Thread-local resources are released at thread exit (Chapters 10, 24).[/li][li]Benchmark datadirs live under the [tt]/srv/nvme8tb[/tt] bind mount, and the journal is read for mounts before a drive is blamed (Chapter 5).[/li][li]Before any timed launch, no process has its working directory under the bench volume (Chapter 25).[/li][li]Every benchmark run has a process sampler beside it on both sides (PSS, heap and CPU), with memory lines at IBD end and at ready. Core #6's peak was lost for want of one.[/li][li]Claim apply speed only with the stage row beside it (Chapter 11).[/li][li]Before calling a run download-bound, check the eviction acks and the block gaps (Chapter 12).[/li][li]Bench rows copy Core's loop shape, or say why not (Chapter 22).[/li][li]Ports from the Mac tree are revert-checked on x86 (Chapter 26).[/li][/list]

Older rules held and were used constantly: a test is untrusted until it has been watched to fail with its fix reverted; never poll a benchmark node; land batches as [tt]--no-ff[/tt] merges; never [tt]git add -A[/tt].

[hr]
[size=14pt][b]PART VIII: WHAT IS STILL OPEN[/b][/size]

[list][li]Memory (Chapter 18): the one category Core keeps, by about 2× in PSS.[/li][li]Two changes built and merged that day but not yet measured: B12, which switches the header download away from a leader that slows, and B13 (#405, open), which bans a peer only for a second window stall. Run 41 banned 34 of 132 peers, 26 of them newly drawn peers that had not finished their first chunk within Core's 2 s timeout. The next benchmark measures both. The targets: block 1 within 50 s of boot on every sync, and five bans or fewer per run.[/li][li]B9 part 1: the ranking's top changes from run to run. 69 to 81 distinct peers served each sync of the release line, against 25 in run 38.[/li][li]A4: one 149.5 s [tt]getmininginfo[/tt] freeze after a restart into catch-up, on 10-03. Per-step timings have been logged since, and it has not recurred.[/li][li]The production unit's descriptor limit is still the default 1,024. Raising it only buys time against a leak. Alerting on the degraded-listener line is the better half and is not done.[/li][li]The Esplora facade still starts a thread per connection.[/li][/list]

The previous report ended with a node that was probably as fast as Core and measuring equipment that could finally say so. This one ends with a node that is 2.6× faster at the job a node is for, where the measurements of where that time goes are trusted enough that a two-second sleep showed up in them. The next question is the one Part VII keeps asking: whether the next fix gets watched long enough, under real traffic, before it is called done.

